# One correction, one line. Six pipe-separated fields, no wrapping:
#
#   DATE | SLUG | CLASS | CHANNEL | GATE | NOTE
#
# DATE     YYYY-MM-DD
# SLUG     short kebab-case handle; reuse one only for a literally recurring problem
# CLASS    from .ai/gates/data/corrections_vocab.json -- which ships EMPTY, so the
#          first entry you write also adds its class to that file
# CHANNEL  who found it: karel-style maintainer name, claude, gate, test, audit, none
# GATE     yes | yes-now | no-judgment | no-gap | n/a
# NOTE     >=40 chars, and length is not the point. Three things earn the entry:
#          what was wrong concretely, WHY IT WAS BELIEVED, and what generalises.
#          The middle one is what makes clustering possible -- the shape of the
#          error is what repeats, not its subject.
#
# Validated by `nightshift.gates.corrections_log` on every edit, so a malformed
# line cannot sit here quietly. Read the distribution with
# `python -m nightshift.corrections`.
#
# This file starts empty and that is the intended state. The framework ships the
# machinery for earning rules, not a set of earned rules.
2026-08-03 | uninstall-deleted-what-it-kept | derived-what-only-the-act-knew | maintainer | no-judgment | `uninstall.plan` decided what to delete by asking `init.build_plan` what it writes and intersecting with the disk, taking `writes | kept` as the candidate set. After a run our own files EXIST, so the second build files them under `kept` -- the same list as a `CLAUDE.md`, `.gitattributes`, `.claude/memory/arch.md` or `docs/tier-binding.md` the project already had and `init` deliberately left alone. So `nightshift uninstall --yes` in any repo that was not empty deleted the operator's own documents. Reproduced 2026-08-03 on a synthetic repo carrying all four: every one appeared under `delete 27 file(s)`. Why it was believed: the intersection is the mechanism that makes the command safe in the ordinary case, and `test_uninstall_removes_exactly_what_init_wrote` passed -- because its fixture is a FRESH repo, where `kept` is empty and the wrong list and the right list are the same list. Every uninstall test used that fixture, so the suite could not see the case. Generalises: DERIVING A FACT FROM THE CURRENT STATE CANNOT RECOVER PROVENANCE -- only the act that wrote a file knows it wrote one, and `init`'s own refusal to overwrite is exactly what guarantees the two are indistinguishable afterwards. Same shape as a fixture that is too clean to contain the bug: the empty case is where the wrong algorithm and the right one agree. Fixed: `init` writes `.ai/install.json` naming what it created (accumulating across re-runs, or a second `init` would leave a receipt saying it made nothing), `uninstall` reads that and intersects it with what `init` can write so a hand-edited receipt is not obeyed, pre-receipt installs fall back to byte-comparison and report what they declined, and nine tests in tests/test_install_flow.py run every case through a repo that already has its own four documents.
2026-08-03 | never-overwrite-read-as-nothing-to-do | derived-what-only-the-act-knew | maintainer | no-gap | The other half of `uninstall-deleted-what-it-kept`, and the reason there was nothing to remove: `init` staged `CLAUDE.md`, `.gitattributes`, the `[tiers].binding_doc` and `Board/README.md` through the same never-overwrite path as everything else, so a project that already had any of them got one line reading 'keep (already present, untouched)' and the requirement was NEVER INSTALLED. For those four the content is the requirement and the file existing is not: the rules a session must follow, the `* text=auto eol=lf` line, the fenced block `nightshift.tiers` parses, the lane contract. Measured 2026-08-03 in a repo with three of them present: two gate violations on the very first run (`line_endings` on a `.gitattributes` that lacked the rule, `dead_code` unrelated) and `tiers.binding()` unable to find a block at the path the manifest pointed at -- an install that reported success and had configured nothing. Why it was believed: 'never overwrite the operator's file' is a real and load-bearing rule, and `stage()` is how it is kept; the error was reading it as 'therefore do nothing', when the third option -- add to the file without touching what is there -- was available the whole time. Every install test used a fixture with none of the four files, so the branch never ran. Generalises: NEVER-OVERWRITE IS NOT THE SAME RULE AS NOTHING-TO-DO, and conflating them turns a safety property into a silent skip. The tell is a report line in the passive voice: 'kept, untouched' names what was not done and never asks whether something still needs doing. Fixed: a `merge()` path appends a block fenced by `nightshift:begin`/`nightshift:end` markers, with the framework's CLAUDE.md half going to `.ai/CLAUDE.md` and a pointer into theirs; `uninstall` strips exactly those blocks by reading the markers, so it is symmetric with or without a receipt; `branch_role_prose` gained the second