2026-08-03 | private-lane-never-created | origin-repo-had-it-by-hand | maintainer | yes-now | `init` created the board by iterating `board.LANES`, and the ideas lane is deliberately NOT in that tuple -- `ideas_fence` derives 'this lane is private' from a board subdirectory being absent from it, which is how the rule gets written down once instead of twice. Correct design, wrong consumer: the installer read the same tuple as 'the lanes that exist', so the maintainer's own lane was the one lane nobody created for them. Meanwhile `Board/README.md` opens with a flow beginning at that lane, the CLAUDE.md template names it as private, and `reconcile` calls itself its only permitted reader -- three documents describing a directory the install never made. It fails open in the quietest way available: `reconcile` globbing a missing directory finds no notes, which is indistinguishable from having none. Reported by the maintainer, 2026-08-03, who simply asked whether it was true. Why it was believed: `LANES` is the right list for every other caller (what may be moved into, what may be scanned, what the digest reports), so reaching for it in a sixth place looked like reuse rather than a category error -- and in the origin project that directory has existed since before the extraction, so no run there could ever have noticed. Second instance of `origin-repo-had-it-by-hand` in one day, after `runtime-output-never-ignored`; the class is earning its place. Generalises: A LIST WITH A PURPOSE IS NOT A LIST OF WHAT EXISTS. When a name is omitted from a collection in order to encode a property, every later caller inherits the omission along with the property -- including the callers that only wanted the enumeration. Fixed: `board.PRIVATE_LANE` owns the string (`reconcile.PRIVATE` and `card_schema`'s literal now point at it), `init` stages that lane on its own line with the reason attached, and `test_init_creates_every_lane_including_the_private_one` asserts SET EQUALITY against `LANES |