2026-08-14 | guards-composed-into-a-prohibition | guards-composed-into-a-prohibition | claude | no-judgment | `commit_pathspec` refuses a `git commit` that does not name its paths (built 2026-07-28 for `commit-swept-staged-index`) and `ideas_fence` refused any Bash command that DID name `Board/ideas/` (built 2026-07-27 for `triage-read-private-ideas`). Each is correct, each is fully unit-tested, and their denial sets intersect to leave the private lane UNCOMMITTABLE: there is no spelling of "commit this note" that satisfies both, so an operation neither guard was written to forbid was forbidden by both, for 17 days, over files that are tracked in git and therefore have to be committed by somebody. Not caught by any gate, test or session -- caught because Karel asked for the capability: "I want you to be able to commit and push cards in ideas folder." That is the diagnostic property of this class: the symptom is a MISSING action, and nothing observes an action nobody attempts. Why it was believed fine: both hooks were reviewed and tested in isolation, which is the right unit for a guard's own correctness and the wrong unit for its effect -- each suite asks "does this deny what it should", and none can ask "what is still possible once every guard has run", because no hook can see the others and no test file has both in scope. The two even cite each other's incidents in their docstrings without either author noticing they now overlapped. Generalises: A GUARD'S BLAST RADIUS IS NOT ITS OWN DENIAL SET BUT THE INTERSECTION OF EVERY GUARD ON THE SAME TOOL, AND THAT INTERSECTION IS VISIBLE FROM NO SINGLE SITE -- so the reviewable question when adding a PreToolUse hook is not only "what does this deny" but "name an operation that must stay possible on the paths it covers, and produce the exact command that still does it". Fixed: ideas_fence lets a Bash call through when EVERY segment is a git invocation whose subcommand only relocates a path (add/commit/push/mv/rm/restore/reset) and carries no content-printing flag -- allowlist not denylist, every segment must be git so a reader riding behind the first command still denies, and command substitution or redirection denies rather than gets parsed. Reading the lane is unchanged and the denial text now names the carve-out, so the next actor does not go hunting for a workaround for something that works. 12 new tests, both directions verified live against the real board. [[disposition: commit: 02d03d4]]
2026-08-14 | project-owner-named-in-a-framework-report | origin-identity-baked-in | test | no-gap | `ingest.report()` labelled the inline bucket with the origin project owner's name -- 'Karel, at the keyboard. No card is written for these.' -- as a heading blurb in a report this module writes into ANY consuming repo, so the first person to install it would read a report addressed to somebody else. Caught by `test_no_runtime_string_names_the_origin_project` on the full-suite run, before the commit, which is the gate working exactly as designed. Why it was believed: the module was authored inside a long session in the origin project, discussing that project's board with its owner, so second person and his name were interchangeable while writing -- and the module's own docstring names him LEGITIMATELY, quoting the decision that produced the money rule, because provenance is what a docstring is for. So the file already contained the name in a correct position, and the incorrect one looked like more of the same. Generalises: WHEN A FILE LEGITIMATELY QUOTES THE ORIGIN PROJECT FOR PROVENANCE, EVERY LATER STRING IN THAT FILE INHERITS THE VOICE. Both are string literals a few lines apart and the difference is not lexical, so re-reading for names cannot separate them; the question that does is WHO READS THIS STRING -- a maintainer of this package, or an operator of some other repo. Same asymmetry the class already names, arriving through authorship rather than through hardcoding. Fixed: the heading is second person, with a comment at the site naming the constraint and the test that enforces it, so the next person editing that dict is told the rule where they would break it rather than in a test file they have no reason to open.
2026-08-14 | generated-view-joined-no-list | category-of-one-read-as-a-literal | claude | no-gap | `Digest.md` was the only report the tooling wrote at the repo root, so its name sat as a bare literal in three places that each need the whole set: `board.commit_board` staged it, `runner.dirty_outside_board` exempted it from the dirty-tree refusal, and `hooks.correction_prompt._IGNORED` discounted its churn. Then `ingest` shipped `Routing.md` and `chores` shipped `Chores.md`, each with its own module-level `OUT = Path("...")`, and joined none of the three lists. Both consequences fired the first time the two commands were run for real, minutes apart, in the origin project on 2026-08-14: routing the inbox wrote `Routing.md`, planning a batch wrote `Chores.md`, and `python -m nightshift.chores` then refused with 'working tree is dirty outside Board/: Chores.md, Routing.md' -- the two new commands blocking each other, and the dispatcher refusing the run that had just produced the files. The second half is quieter and was never observed: a view that is not staged exists on one machine only, which for a routing decision or a play checklist is board state the other machine cannot see. Why it was believed: with one member there is no set to notice the absence of -- `"Digest.md"` reads as correct at all three sites and the repetition looks like coincidence rather than a missing constant, so a second and third view were added by writing the same kind of line the first one had and nothing pointed at the consumers. The tests could not see it either: every one named `Digest.md` literally, so all three lists stayed green while being wrong about two thirds of their subject. Generalises: A CATEGORY WITH ONE MEMBER IS A LITERAL, AND ITS SECOND MEMBER ARRIVES WITHOUT A REFERENCE TO FOLLOW. The tell is the same string appearing in three modules that do not otherwise know about each other -- and the fix is to name the category the FIRST time, when it looks like over-engineering. Also that a commit list and an exemption list over the same set must be the same object: committed-but-not-exempt blocks every dispatch, exempt-but-not-committed loses the file. Fixed: `board.GENERATED_VIEWS` (with `DIGEST_VIEW`/`ROUTING_VIEW`/`CHORES_VIEW`) owns the set, the three writers derive their `OUT` from it, all three consumers read the tuple, and six tests in tests/test_runner.py are parametrised over the tuple so a fourth view is covered the day it is added rather than the day someone remembers these three sites.
2026-08-14 | effort-cap-measured-the-repo | derived-not-verified | maintainer | n/a | The chore batch carried a 40-turn / 20-minute effort budget as its mechanical backstop on "was this actually a one-prompter", and an item that exceeded it was failed as "not a one-prompter", dropped from the batch and left on its branch. First contact with real work broke both halves at once: `tools-console-encoding` took 48 turns, went green on the gates and on every test reaching its diff, and was bounced -- for a two-line edit in two files plus one new gate and an audit row. A turn is one assistant step that used a tool, so the count is dominated by how many files must be opened to be sure and how often the suite runs; the same attempt read 3.4M tokens from cache in a ~1,900-test project. Maintainer, shown the number: "the question is if the work is straightforward (=simple) not if it needs X turns or modify X files." The second defect is worse and was invisible while the first was believed: the cap was read AFTER the worker finished, so it could no longer prevent anything -- the time was already spent, and the only act left to it was discarding a gated, tested result. It then parked that result in `review/`, where nothing would ever pick it up: the batch's single review runs over the MERGED diff and the item never reached the merge, while both the batch selector and the night take their queue from `tasks/`. So a correct diff sat in a lane with no reviewer scheduled and nothing to report that it was stuck. Why it was believed: the plan that specified it argued explicitly for measuring effort rather than output, and that argument is right against the thing it was aimed at -- a line-count cap, which the same paragraph rejects because 200 mechanical lines are trivial and a four-line off-by-one is not. "Effort, not output" is a real improvement on "output", and being an improvement is what stopped anyone asking whether effort tracks simplicity either. It does not: it tracks FRICTION, which is a property of the codebase. Generalises: A MEASUREMENT THAT IMPROVES ON A WORSE MEASUREMENT IS STILL NOT A VALIDATED ONE -- and a threshold read after the work is done cannot protect anything, only destroy something. Ask what a cap is still able to prevent at the moment it is evaluated; if the answer is nothing, it is not a guard. The runaway it was meant to stop was already bounded where stopping works, by the wall-clock timeout on the worker's own process, which `runner`'s own comment names. Fixed: the budget is gone, `effort_exceeded` is now `cost_note` which returns prose for the report and no verdict any caller could branch on, a green chore lands however many turns it took, the worker prompt's effort paragraph now asks what it still does not know rather than how long it has taken, and two tests pin it -- one that 500 turns still lands, one that a green chore never ends in a lane no reviewer drains. Left open and reported separately: `review/` has no automated drain at all, so `_hand_over`'s batch-did-not-land path still relies on a human noticing.
2026-08-15 | account-veto-checked-config-only-not-live-signal | derived-not-verified | claude | yes-now | `panel.py`'s `_guard_dispatch_account` (built for item 14 of a consuming project's dispatch-cost plan, the Command Center) first checked only the configured `[[accounts]]` `dispatch:` stance before allowing a POST to /api/dispatch. Running the panel live against a real board with no `[[accounts]]` entries at all yet showed the rail correctly rendering "API spend ENABLED on this account" from `usage.read_identity()`, while the guard itself never read that signal at all: a dispatch to the one account `feedback_account_dispatch` exists to protect would have gone through and spawned a real `python -m nightshift.runner --card <id>` subprocess. Why believed: the design doc's own prose already says the live `hasExtraUsageEnabled` signal "may veto an account config forgot to mark," and that exact sentence was read and paraphrased into the guard's own docstring before the config-stance half was implemented and tested against synthetic fixtures with an account already populated -- so the check felt complete the moment the half with a config object to point at was done, and the half needing no config at all to exist was never written. Fixed by having the guard also veto on `identity.has_extra_usage_enabled`, unconditionally, and two tests now pin it: one asserting the veto fires with zero accounts configured, one asserting it does not fire on a clean identity read. Generalises: A RULE STATED IN TWO HALVES OF ONE SENTENCE CAN SHIP WITH ONLY THE HALF THAT HAS A CONFIG OBJECT TO CHECK -- the half that is a live read requiring no configuration is easy to forget precisely because there is no fixture whose absence would remind you, so a guard's own test suite must include the zero-config case, not only the pre-populated one. [[disposition: commit: ai/command-center-panel]]
2026-08-16 | live-pid-is-not-a-live-run | derived-not-verified | claude | yes-now | `panel.py`'s status rail asked one question to decide whether a run was in flight -- is the pid in `.ai/runs/status.json` alive -- and rendered "RUNNING NOW", a pulsing dot, the phase pills and a climbing elapsed counter when it said yes. Screenshotting the real board showed `tools-console-encoding` running for 2482 minutes, 41 hours, on a machine where nothing had dispatched for two days. The heartbeat was from 2026-08-14 and its pid, 57932, had since been recycled by the OS to an unrelated `claude.exe` -- so the check was not merely stale-tolerant, it was answering a question about a completely different process and reporting the answer as fact about this one. Why it was believed: `runner.print_status` does exactly this and its own docstring is emphatic about the case it does handle ("NOT RUNNING -- stale, from a run that ended or died"), so the pattern read as the framework's settled answer to the question and was reused rather than re-derived. It handles a *cleared* pid; nothing in it handles a *reused* one, and pid reuse on a developer box that starts hundreds of processes a day is ordinary rather than exotic. Also worth naming: nothing clears `status.json` on the way out, so the file's mere existence proves nothing and the last phase of every finished run sits there forever looking current. Fixed with three signals, cheapest last: a finished run record supersedes the heartbeat outright; a heartbeat older than a two-hour trust window is not believed (past any legitimate phase, since the worker's own wall-clock timeout is an hour); and only then is the pid consulted. Five tests pin it, including one that asserts the pid IS alive so that the age check is provably the thing doing the work. Generalises: A PID IS A HANDLE, NOT AN IDENTITY -- the OS reuses it, so "is this pid alive" answers a question about whoever holds it now, and any check that treats it as proof that a *particular* process still runs needs a second signal the process itself wrote (a timestamp, a completion record) to tie the handle back to the thing it was meant to name. Left open deliberately: `runner.print_status` has the same defect and would report the same 41-hour phantom; it is the CLI's own surface and belongs to a separate change rather than being fixed in passing from the panel's branch. [[disposition: commit: ai/panel-front-end]]
2026-08-16 | second-parser-kept-the-first-ones-blind-spot | derived-not-verified | claude | yes-now | `board.parse_fields` -- the board's own card model, which the runner, the digest and reconcile all read through -- could not see YAML's block-list form: `tags:` followed by indented `- item` lines matched no `key: value` line, so the field came back as the empty string and the items were skipped entirely. `card_schema._frontmatter` has folded that form since 2026-08-06 (`tag-parser-blind-to-block-form`), which means for ten weeks the model and the gate checking it returned different answers about the same bytes, and the model's answer was the wrong one. Found while adding a `nightshift` tag chip to the panel: the chip never appeared, and the card plainly had the tag. Why it was believed: the fix for `tag-parser-blind-to-block-form` was applied where the *symptom* was -- a gate reporting green on a card it should have failed -- and `card_schema` keeps an independent copy of the frontmatter grammar **on purpose**, documented in its own docstring, because a gate must stay importable with no siblings on the path. So the duplication was known, justified and left in place, and nobody asked whether the OTHER copy had the same hole; the justified copy got the fix and the canonical one did not. Generalises: WHEN A SECOND PARSER IS KEPT DELIBERATELY, A FIX TO EITHER COPY IS A FINDING ABOUT BOTH -- the reason for the duplication (isolation, startup cost) is never a reason the input grammar differs, so the question after patching one is always 'which other copy reads this same file', and the answer must be checked rather than assumed. Fixed in `parse_fields` itself, with `Card.tags` added on top and four tests pinning that block and inline forms parse identically and that the field after a block list is not swallowed. [[disposition: commit: ai/panel-review-round-2]]
2026-08-16 | degradation-verified-on-some-paths-claimed-for-all | derived-not-verified | claude | yes-now | The design for a panel-first install rested on one asserted fact, written into the approved plan as "the enabling fact": that `panel.py` already renders in a repo with no `.ai/`, so `bootstrap` could write the Command Center launchers BEFORE `init` and the panel could then host its own install. The claim was checked by reading three reads and finding all three degrade -- `manifest.find_root` falls back to `.git/`, `board.board_root` catches `ManifestError` and defaults to `Board`, `_accounts` catches it too -- and every one of those readings is correct. `read_context` also calls `default_base(root)`, which goes through `branches.integration` to `manifest.require`, and that one raises ON PURPOSE, because every card is built on whatever it returns and the field is never guessed. So the panel died on the first request in exactly the repo the whole design existed to serve, and the failure was one line of traceback away from a feature nobody could have used. Why it was believed: the three reads that were checked are the ones a reader reaches for when asking "does this survive a missing manifest" -- they are the board-shaped reads, and they are where a degradation would be written if someone had thought about it, so finding graceful handling in all three read as evidence that the question had already been considered module-wide rather than as three independent coincidences. It also flatters the reading: each `except ManifestError` is a small proof that the author had this case in mind, and three proofs feel like a policy. `default_base` is not board-shaped, sits behind a helper whose name suggests a default rather than a requirement, and its raise is a deliberate refusal rather than an oversight -- so it is both easy to miss and correct as written. Generalises: A DEGRADATION CLAIM IS ABOUT AN ENTRY POINT, NOT ABOUT THE CALLS YOU HAPPENED TO READ -- sampling call sites can only ever disprove it, never establish it, and the sample is biased because you reach for the calls where degradation would be written. The only evidence is running the entry point in the degraded state, which costs one throwaway directory and takes seconds. The tell that this reasoning is happening at all: the phrase "already survives" in a plan, resting on a list rather than on a transcript. Fixed by making the property real instead of assumed -- `read_context` now returns early with an empty context when the repo has no manifest, and `test_the_panel_renders_in_a_repo_with_no_install` runs both `/system` and `/now` against a bootstrap-only repo so the claim is executed on every suite run rather than re-argued. [[disposition: commit: ai/command-center-front-door]]
2026-08-16 | shared-marker-could-not-say-which-command-wrote-it | derived-what-only-the-act-knew | claude | yes-now | `panel.installed()` decided whether to offer the install or the maintenance page by asking whether `.ai/install.json` exists, on the reasoning that the receipt is written by an install and therefore proves one happened. It is written by `nightshift bootstrap` too: `bootstrap_plan` stages the install skill and the two launchers through the same `Plan`, deliberately, so that `uninstall` can take those files back like anything else. The consequence was precise and self-cancelling -- a repo that had bootstrapped and nothing else reported `installed = True`, so the Setup button was hidden from the only repo that needed it, and the page then failed rendering on the manifest that had never been written. Why it was believed: the receipt genuinely IS the authority for the question `uninstall` asks ("did we create this file, and may it be deleted"), that authority is argued at length in `receipt_text`'s own docstring, and the new question sounded like the same question. It is not: "did nightshift write anything here" and "is nightshift configured here" have identical answers for every repo except the one in the middle of being set up, and that repo is the entire subject of the page. Generalises: A MARKER THAT MORE THAN ONE COMMAND WRITES CANNOT ANSWER WHICH ONE RAN, and the reuse is most tempting when a well-argued docstring has established the marker as authoritative -- for a different question. Ask what the marker's existence literally proves, not what it usually accompanies; if two commands can produce it, it distinguishes neither. Fixed by keying on `.ai/manifest.toml`, which only `init` writes and which every branch, board and gate read resolves through, so its absence means none of them can answer -- with `test_bootstrap_does_not_claim_the_repo_is_installed` asserting the receipt exists and `installed()` is still False. [[disposition: commit: ai/command-center-front-door]]
2026-08-16 | absence-from-the-template-read-as-death | derived-not-verified | claude | yes-now | `update._survey_settings` converged `.claude/settings.json`'s hook entries by stripping every hook whose command contains `nightshift.` (reusing `uninstall.strip_hooks`, which is correct for an uninstall) and then merging today's template fragment back. That converges, and it silently deletes any hook the CONSUMING PROJECT added that runs through this package. Not hypothetical: the very first real repo it was pointed at reported "1 hook entry to remove -- a renamed module leaves a hook firing nothing" about `python -m nightshift.hooks.project_script .ai/recipes/hint.py`, a live hook whose script exists, whose module exists, and which that project added on purpose. `project_script` is in no template BY DESIGN -- it is the documented escape hatch that lets a project reach its own script from any working directory, added after `hook-command-resolved-against-a-moved-cwd`. Why it was believed: strip-then-merge is genuinely the only pass that CONVERGES, which is a real argument and was the whole reason for the design -- `init` can only add, so a hook whose module was renamed sits in the file forever firing nothing, and nothing else removes it. Having solved the convergence problem correctly, the question of what the strip half was allowed to take was never asked separately; and the two halves were tested together against a fixture whose only nightshift hooks came from the template, so "everything ours" and "everything the template declares" were the same set in every test. The report even names the right consequence ("a renamed module leaves a hook firing nothing") while the code was not checking for a renamed module at all -- it was checking for absence from the fragment. Generalises: WHEN A FIX IS FOR X, CHECK FOR X -- deadness here is a property of the module (does it still resolve), not of the template (do we ship it), and the two coincide only in a repo that has never used the extension points the framework provides. A convergent algorithm answers "what should the file end up containing"; it does not license the deletion set it takes to get there. Fixed: `update.dead_hook` resolves the module named in the command with `importlib.util.find_spec` -- no import, no execution -- so a renamed module is removed and a live one is left alone whoever added it; `uninstall.strip_hooks` grew a `predicate` parameter so the traversal is not copied, with `is_ours` still the default and still correct for uninstall (the package is going away, so a hook running through it goes too). Two tests pin it, one reproducing the real hook verbatim. [[disposition: commit: ai/command-center-front-door]]
2026-08-17 | preflight-pytest-could-hang-forever | unbounded-wait-read-as-progress | maintainer | yes-now | `preflight._run_subset` launched pytest with `subprocess.run(..., capture_output=True)` and no `timeout`, so a suite that HUNG rather than failed produced no output, no exit and no end. It bit twice on 2026-08-16 in a consuming project whose full suite normally takes 199 s: both runs sat for 14+ minutes and would never have finished. Diagnosed in an earlier session with py-spy: every xdist worker parked in `run_one_test -> get()` on a `threading.Event` waiting for the controller, the controller parked in `loop_once -> queue.get()` waiting for a worker, nothing executing, no child processes and no sockets. Process start times gave the trigger both times -- one worker started ~95 s after its siblings, i.e. the original died and xdist respawned it, and the session never recovered from the replacement. Load-correlated: `probed_worker_count()` reads free PHYSICAL memory (not commit, which was fine at 73%), and a re-run minutes later with one more worker passed all 1937 tests. Why the gap was believed absent: the framework already KNOWS this can happen -- `runner._run_tests` takes a `timeout` parameter and `merge_check` catches `subprocess.TimeoutExpired` and reports "pytest: timed out after Ns" -- so the hazard was understood and guarded at two of the three call sites. Preflight was written later, against the same `suite` module, and reads as the careful one: it shares selection, parallelisation and the JUnit verdict with the runner, and that visible sharing is exactly what made the ONE thing it did not share invisible. Nobody compares a caller against its siblings for what they have and it lacks; you compare it against what it needs to do, and it did all of that. Generalises: WHEN SIBLING CALLERS OF ONE MECHANISM DIFFER, THE ODD ONE OUT IS A FINDING, NOT A STYLE CHOICE -- and specifically, any subprocess that can block on a resource must carry a ceiling, because the failure of an unbounded wait is indistinguishable from success-in-progress and therefore trains the operator to wait. Second-order lesson worth as much: the cheap discriminator between hung and slow is `py-spy dump --pid <controller>` -- all-idle stacks mean deadlock -- and sampling CPU over 20 s (0.1 s consumed = stalled) needs no tooling at all. Fixed: preflight's pytest run takes `[tests].timeout_s` or a 2400 s default, and on expiry fails with a message naming the deadlock, the py-spy command and the fact that a re-run usually succeeds. Three tests pin it. NOT fixed and deliberately left open: which test kills a worker in the first place -- the session dies before writing junit, re-runs are green, and the precedent (`mid-run-set-mode-crashed-a-worker`, that project's log, 2026-08-07) records a pygame `set_mode` crashing a worker outright without root-causing it either. [[disposition: commit: preflight timeout + the py-spy discriminator recorded here]]
2026-08-17 | discarded-output-read-as-nothing-happened | reported-into-a-channel-nobody-read | maintainer | no-gap | `panel.spawn_background` started every long-running verb with `stdout=DEVNULL, stderr=DEVNULL` and kept no record that it had run, so a command's entire account of itself was discarded at the moment of starting it. Measured 2026-08-17: the maintainer pressed Classify all, reported that it 'either does nothing or communicates poorly', and the command had in fact succeeded completely -- thirteen notes routed, `Routing.md` rewritten at 09:51. Three surfaces failed at once and each is the same shape. Every line `ingest` prints went to DEVNULL: the meters, `classifying with sonnet ...`, the per-route counts, and, on the refusal path, `REFUSED before classifying`. The rail's only in-progress surface reads `.ai/runs/status.json`, which nothing but `runner` writes, so it went on reporting a two-day-old heartbeat while a live process ran. And the one message that did reach the browser -- the toast naming the pid -- was destroyed 900 ms later by the `location.reload()` that every successful action fires, against a toast timer of five seconds. That last one is worse than symmetrical: a REFUSED verb does not reload, so failures stayed on screen and successes did not, which teaches the reading that the panel answers only when it has bad news. Why it was believed: the docstring's argument is sound as far as it goes -- 'the HTTP request returns immediately; progress is read from the files the command itself already writes (.ai/runs/status.json, the run records), never from this function holding the connection open' -- and every clause of it is true OF THE RUNNER, whose files those are and on whose dispatch path the function was written. It was then reused for `ingest`, `chores`, `drain`, `preflight`, `fix` and `update --merge`, none of which writes a status file at all. The claim was never wrong about the code; it was about a different command, and reuse carried the code without carrying the precondition. Generalises: DISCARDING A STREAM IS A CLAIM THAT SOMETHING ELSE REPORTS, AND THAT CLAIM IS ABOUT A NAMED WRITER, NOT A MECHANISM -- before sending output to DEVNULL, say which file the reader will read and which command writes it, per command rather than per code path, because a shared spawn helper is exactly where per-command preconditions go to die. The corollary caught the second half: a message displayed for five seconds by code that reloads in one is also a report with no reader, and the two defects were invisible to each other. Fixed: `nightshift.jobs` writes a record before the spawn, hands the log file to a wrapper process that outlives the panel and writes the exit code back in a `finally`, and the status rail carries a line per background command with a link to its output; the toast is parked in sessionStorage and re-shown by the page that replaces it. Third instance of the same class found in the same pass and fixed with it: the inbox page listed all thirteen routed notes as 'Not yet classified', because routing is a view and a note never leaves the lane -- the routing was in a file the page linked by name and timestamp without ever reading. Not fixed and deliberately left open: a gate could forbid `stdout=DEVNULL` in the tooling dirs, which would have caught this mechanically, but the honest scope of such a gate needs the second instance to know where its exceptions are. [[disposition: commit: ai/panel-background-visibility]]
2026-08-17 | route-accepted-everywhere-consumed-nowhere | schema-field-nothing-read | maintainer | no-gap | The `chore` route was a dead end for as long as it existed, and every part of it except the connecting call was built. `ROUTES` accepted the value; the classifier's charter defines it ('these become thin cards and run in a batch overnight. Most short notes are chores'); `report()` gave it its own heading and blurb in the routing view ('Chores - batch overnight: Thin cards, verified as one batch'); the scribe's charter has a whole section on writing one, headed `kind: chore` - when the note is a one-prompter; `card_schema` relaxes exactly one section for the kind; `chores.select` reads `kind: chore` cards out of `tasks/` and batches them. The only thing missing was that `ingest --scribe` fanned over `by_route('scribe')` alone, so nothing ever asked the scribe for a chore card. Consequence: a chore-routed note sat in `inbox/` forever, and the panel's Chores section was permanently empty AND permanently accurate. Found by Karel asking why, after a classify pass had routed three notes to `chore`, the board showed no chores - not by anything failing. Why it was believed: the route's acceptance by the vocabulary was read as evidence of its wiring, and here that evidence was unusually thick - a value in `ROUTES`, a section in two agent charters, a heading in the generated report, a relaxation in the schema gate, and a consumer that batches them. Every one of those is a place someone thought about chores; none of them is the call that produces one. The report is the worst of the six, because it prints the heading with a count of zero next to the notes it just routed there, which reads as an empty bucket rather than as a bucket with no outlet. Generalises: A ROUTE IS ONLY REAL WHERE SOMETHING CONSUMES IT, AND EVERY OTHER MENTION IS DECORATION - so for each value of an enum, name the call that acts on it, one per value, and treat a value whose consumer you cannot name as unimplemented no matter how much prose surrounds it. The tell is a fan-out that filters the enum by a literal (`by_route('scribe')`) rather than by a set the enum itself declares. Fixed: `WRITABLE_ROUTES` is the set, both members are fanned over, and the two that stay out carry the reason they stay out (`inline` gets no card by definition; `triage` is never dispatched from here). Found and fixed with it, same branch, different shape: nothing consumed a note when its card was written - logged against the charter half in the consuming project's own log as `charter-specified-content-not-destination`. [[disposition: commit: ingest.WRITABLE_ROUTES + the per-route actions on the panel's own rows]]
2026-08-17 | wrote-the-card-committed-nothing | delegated-the-write-kept-none-of-its-bookkeeping | claude | no-gap | `ingest.scribe` wrote cards and committed none of them. Every neighbouring board write in the framework commits: each `boardcmd` verb ends in `board.commit_board`, `board.move` commits the move, `chores` commits as it lands each item, the runner commits its own card moves so that a night is resumable from disk. This one path did not, so the note's deletion and the new card sat in the working tree indefinitely. Measured 2026-08-17 on the first real fan-out: `skills-tinkering` had been a correctly written card for an hour and `git status` still showed `D Board/inbox/skills-tinkering.md` beside an untracked `Board/tasks/skills-tinkering.md`. `Routing.md` had the same problem for the same reason and had been modified-uncommitted since 09:51, indistinguishable from a hand edit. Why it was believed: on every other path the code that WRITES is the code that COMMITS, so the commit is written next to the write and nobody has to remember it separately. Here the write was delegated -- the scribe edits the note in place and moves it, which is the correct design and the whole reason the agent exists -- and the commit went with the write rather than staying with the caller. The agent was never told to commit (it should not be: an agent committing is an agent deciding what a transaction is), and the caller had no line touching the files to hang it off. Both halves are complete in their own terms, which is why reading either one finds nothing missing. Generalises: WHEN A WRITE IS DELEGATED, ITS BOOKKEEPING DOES NOT GO WITH IT -- so the caller inherits a step it never had, and the way to find it is to compare siblings on the RECORD rather than on the work: every other path here produces a commit, this one produced a file. Fixed: `ingest._commit` closes the transaction per note rather than per run (a fan-out that loses its window partway would otherwise leave every card it did write uncommitted, making the cheapest failure cost the most work), with distinct messages for a card, a bounce re-route and a stranded half-transition -- the last committed deliberately, because it is already on disk and leaving it dirty only means the next board write sweeps it up under an unrelated message. The classify pass commits its own view for the same reason. Seven tests, and the ingest fixture is now a real git repo: `commit_board` warns rather than raising, on purpose, so a gitless fixture would have let this regress while every test went on passing. [[disposition: commit: ingest._commit on every board-changing outcome]]
2026-08-17 | shared-dispatch-missing-the-writers-permissions | reported-into-a-channel-nobody-read | maintainer | no-gap | `ingest._dispatch` built its `claude -p` argv without `--permission-mode`, so the scribe could not write the file it was told to write. Every dispatch site in `runner` passes it - `run_producer`, the checker, the reviewer at line 2258, the drain - all of them as `host_setting(root, 'permission_mode', 'acceptEdits')`; this module passed `-p --agent --output-format json --model` and nothing else. A headless session in the default permission mode has nobody to approve an edit, so the write was refused, the agent explained itself in prose, and the CLI exited 0. Measured 2026-08-17: two notes stranded on one run and the same two again on the next, each reported as 'no card appeared and the note is untouched - nothing happened'. Adding the one flag fixed it on the first attempt: ad-sound-for-recharge carded, note moved, committed. Why the gap was invisible: the classifier runs through the same function and works perfectly, because reading files needs no approval - so the seam had a passing caller and a failing one, and the passing one is the one that runs first and every time. The failure also arrives disguised as a JUDGMENT: an agent that cannot write says so in words, and 'the scribe returned without writing a card' reads as the scribe declining, which is a legitimate outcome this module already models (the bounce). Two of the three explanations offered for it that day - a too-strict bounce criterion, a charter that never stated the file contract - were real defects found and fixed on their own merits, which made the remaining silence look explained. Generalises: WHEN ONE FUNCTION DISPATCHES SEVERAL AGENTS, THE READ-ONLY ONE PROVES NOTHING ABOUT THE WRITING ONE - the permission posture is per-capability, so a shared dispatch helper needs the flags of its most privileged caller, and the way to find the gap is to diff the argv against a sibling caller rather than to reason about the agent's behaviour. Second-order and worth as much: the CLI reports `permission_denials` in its own envelope, `runner.read_telemetry` has read that field for months and prints 'the worker was refused a tool it asked for', and this module discarded it - so the one machine-readable signal that would have named the cause in one line was already on the wire and thrown away. Fixed all three ways: the flag is passed through `host_setting`, `denials()` reads the envelope and says so, and a stranded dispatch now quotes the agent's own reply into the log instead of printing only that nothing happened. [[disposition: commit: --permission-mode in ingest._dispatch, denials() on the envelope, and the reply quoted on a stranded dispatch]]
2026-08-17 | cannot-edit-was-a-literal-in-every-caller | category-of-one-read-as-a-literal | claude | yes-now | Auditing whether `--permission-mode` was missing anywhere else turned up the opposite shape: the flag was present at all seven dispatch sites, and the CHECK around it was a literal in two of them. `update.merge` refused a merge with `if permission_mode == 'default'` and the sentence 'which cannot edit files - the agent could read both versions and write neither' - which is a precise description of the `ingest` failure found earlier the same day, written months before it. `plan` cannot edit either, and would have been dispatched. Worse, I wrote the identical omission into `ingest.cannot_card` an hour before, as `_CANNOT_EDIT = ('default',)`, having just read `update.merge` as the model to copy. Why it was believed: while `default` was the only member, 'a mode that cannot edit' and the string `'default'` were indistinguishable, so spelling out the string was not a shortcut - it was the whole category, correctly expressed. Nothing in either call site hints that a set exists, so there is no reference for a reader to follow and no place for a second member to be added; every consumer has to be recalled from memory instead, which is how I reproduced the bug while looking straight at it. Generalises: A CATEGORY WITH ONE MEMBER IS INVISIBLE, AND COPYING A CORRECT-LOOKING CHECK PROPAGATES IT - so when adding a member to an implicit set, the work is not 'fix this comparison', it is 'find the set, name it, and convert every comparison to ask the set'. The tell is a literal string compared with `==` where the prose around it describes a class of things. Fixed: `runner.MODES_WITHOUT_EDIT` and `runner.cannot_edit()` are the reference, both consumers ask it, and a test asserts that neither module contains `permission_mode == "default"` again. Enumerated rather than inverted on purpose - a mode the CLI adds later fails open, because a verb refusing to start on an unrecognised string is worse than one that tries and reports. Left alone deliberately: `fix.can_dispatch` requires `bypassPermissions` exactly, which is a different question (it needs Bash, not edits) and is already a whitelist rather than a blacklist. [[disposition: commit: runner.MODES_WITHOUT_EDIT + cannot_edit(), asked by ingest and update]]
2026-08-17 | launcher-trusted-path-while-the-resolver-sat-next-door | ambient-environment-assumed-not-resolved | maintainer | no-gap | `panel.open_terminal` spawned `cmd /c start "Command Center" cmd /k claude ...`, passing the bare name and leaving resolution to the new console's PATH. On the maintainer's box `claude.exe` lives in `%USERPROFILE%\.local\bin`, which is not on the persisted user PATH (checked 2026-08-17: the User PATH holds five entries, none of them `.local\bin`; the VS Code extension launches the CLI by absolute path, so nothing else had ever needed it there), so EVERY launcher button on the page -- Start session, Talk, Triage, Setup, Switch account -- opened a window whose entire content was `'claude' is not recognized as an internal or external command`. Reported as 'I tried to start a session from NOW - NOTES ROUTED TO YOU and it looks like it's not working', with the window's own text in a screenshot. The framework has owned the answer for months: `runner.claude_binary()` checks `$CLAUDE_BIN`, then PATH, then `~/.local/bin/claude[.exe]`, and its docstring names this box's layout explicitly -- 'on this box it lives in %USERPROFILE%\.local\bin. Checked rather than assumed, because a runner that discovers this at 3 AM has already moved a card and bumped attempts'. Four dispatch sites in `runner`, plus `ingest`, `night`, `update.merge`, `fix` and `doctor`, all ask it; `doctor` refuses even to run a second `shutil.which` on the grounds that two answers to 'where is the CLI' is one too many. Why the gap was invisible: every caller that asks the resolver asks because it must -- it needs the string to put in an argv it builds itself -- and this function builds no argv for the CLI, it hands a command line to a shell, so the resolution never appeared as a step it was skipping. Handing work to a shell reads as delegation rather than as an assumption. The failure then lands in the one place this code deliberately does not look: the `gate-ok(subprocess_result_checked)` comment on that very line argues, correctly, that there is nothing to be done with the exit code of a visible window nobody has interacted with yet -- so the panel reported success, the toast said 'opened a terminal in this repo', and it had. Generalises: DELEGATING A COMMAND TO A SHELL DELEGATES ITS NAME RESOLUTION TO AN ENVIRONMENT YOU DID NOT INSPECT -- where a repo owns a resolver for an executable, every occurrence of that executable's name is a call site, including the ones that assemble no argv, and a spawn whose exit code is deliberately unread must resolve BEFORE spawning because no channel remains afterwards. The tell is a bare executable name as a string literal in a repo that also contains a `*_binary()` function; the mechanical form of the check is cheap enough that `no-gap` here is a gap worth closing. Fixed: `open_terminal` resolves `claude` through `claude_binary()` and raises `PanelError` -- which the page renders as a sentence -- instead of opening a window that cannot work; three tests pin the resolution, the refusal, and the fact that a non-`claude` command is passed through untouched. Found with it and fixed on the same line: the POSIX branch joined the command with `" ".join`, which would split a resolved path containing spaces and was already splitting the triage prompt into a dozen arguments -- `shlex.join` now. [[disposition: commit: claude_binary() in panel.open_terminal + shlex.join on the POSIX branch]]
2026-08-17 | start-session-was-a-launcher-that-launched-nothing | label-described-the-mechanism-not-the-job | maintainer | no-gap | Three rows on the panel's Now page offered `Start session`, all three posted the same empty `/api/session`, and all three opened a bare `claude` in the repo. The label was exactly true and that was the defect: it described what the code did instead of what the row was for, so nothing about the row reached the session. Karel, 2026-08-17, on the first working click: 'it opens Claude CLI. I need to login and insert a prompt. It is not related to the card, nor my actually used account and I guess the model is default too. This helps very little.' Four misses, and the caller already held all four: the card id (the row IS a card), the model (the card declares `tier:` and `tiers.resolve` binds it), the charter (the card declares `worker:`), and the account (`_ACCOUNT.config_dir`, which `spawn_background` passes as `env=_dispatch_env()` and this function did not pass at all -- so the panel's account dropdown was decoration for every button that opens a terminal). The CLI takes every one of them on the command line, and a positional prompt starts an INTERACTIVE session rather than a `-p` dispatch, so there was never a capability gap to close -- only arguments nobody passed. Why it was believed: the endpoint is honest and the docstring's charter is right -- 'it is a launcher, a registry and a tail, never a chat client' -- and 'open a terminal in this repo' is a complete, correct description of a launcher. What that framing hides is that a launcher's whole value is the arguments it composes; strip those and you have a shortcut to a shell prompt, which the maintainer already had. The generic name is also what let one handler serve three different rows without anyone noticing they had different needs: a label promising only the mechanism can be shared honestly by any caller, and sharing it is what guaranteed no caller passed its own context. Generalises: NAME A CONTROL FOR THE JOB, NOT THE MECHANISM, AND THE MISSING ARGUMENTS BECOME VISIBLE -- 'Work on this' cannot be wired to an empty endpoint without the gap being obvious on sight, where 'Start session' can. The mechanical tell is one parameterless handler reached from rows that each carry an id; a per-row action whose POST body is `{}` is either wrong or badly named. Fixed: `session_argv` composes name, model (via `tiers.resolve`, the same call the runner makes), agent, permission mode and the board `--add-dir`; `_work_verb` builds the prompt from the card or the note and `Work on this` replaces all three labels; `open_terminal` passes `env=_dispatch_env()` like every other spending path. One general session button survives, on the section bar rather than a row, because it is the one launch that genuinely carries nothing. Decided against, on the same evidence: proxying `-p --input-format stream-json` into a browser chat in the panel -- it would reimplement permission prompts, diff rendering and interrupt, and `--remote-control` already mirrors a locally-running session to claude.ai/code, so the charter line holds. Second-order and deliberately NOT inherited: the interactive session reads `interactive_permission_mode` (default `acceptEdits`) rather than the runner's `permission_mode`, which on this box is `bypassPermissions` -- that value answers 'what may a worker do when there is nobody to ask', and reusing it would have handed every session the panel opens fewer guardrails than the same person gets in their editor. [[disposition: commit: session_argv + api/work + Work on this on every row]]
2026-08-17 | prompt-swallowed-by-a-variadic-flag | derived-not-verified | maintainer | yes-now | `session_argv` appended the card prompt as a bare trailing token, immediately after `--add-dir <board>`. `--add-dir` is declared `<directories...>` -- VARIADIC -- so it consumed the prompt as a second directory, and the session opened with an empty input box: right name, right permission mode, remote control up, card nowhere. Karel, on the first click of the shipped button: 'I'm supposed to instruct it to start working at the card?' Reproduced in one line, 2026-08-17: `claude -p --add-dir <dir> "Reply with exactly: ALPHA"` exits with 'Input must be provided either through stdin or as a prompt argument when using --print', and the identical line with `--` inserted answers ALPHA. Why it was believed: the whole change had just been reasoned out from `claude --help` and the one ordering hazard it names was checked and handled -- `--remote-control [name]` takes an OPTIONAL positional, so `--name` was placed immediately after it so the name could not be eaten. Having found and fixed one argument-swallowing hazard, I stopped looking for the second, which was three lines further down the same argv and in the same class. `<directories...>` is right there in the help text I had read. Worse, the flags were verified with `claude --remote-control --name x --model sonnet ... --version`, which parsed cleanly and proved nothing about the prompt: `--version` short-circuits before any positional is read, so the check exercised every token EXCEPT the one that was broken. And nine tests passed, because every one of them asserted the prompt was IN the argv -- and it was, as a directory path. Generalises: A CHECK THAT THE ARGUMENT IS PRESENT IS NOT A CHECK THAT IT ARRIVES, and for a CLI the only honest end-to-end test is to run the real binary on the real argv and have it echo back what it received. Corollary worth as much: `--version`/`--help` short-circuit, so they can only ever prove that parsing did not hard-error -- never that a positional landed. And the specific reusable rule: when an argv ends with a positional, END OPTION PARSING WITH `--` rather than ordering the flags so a single-value one happens to come last; four of the flags available here (`--add-dir`, `--tools`, `--allowed-tools`, `--plugin-dir`) are variadic, so the ordering fix is correct by luck and stops being correct the next time a flag is added. Fixed: `session_argv` emits `["--", prompt]`, `api/triage` passes its prompt THROUGH `session_argv` instead of appending to the list it returns (the same defect, one caller over -- it had dropped the note for one commit), and three tests pin the fence: the prompt is at `argv[-1]` behind a `--` at `argv[-2]`, no launcher that sends a prompt omits it, and a session with no prompt emits no dangling `--`. Verified the way it should have been the first time: the exact argv the button builds, replayed against the real CLI headlessly, asked to echo the card id it was given -- it did. [[disposition: commit: `--` before the prompt in session_argv, and the echo-back replay as the verification for anything that hands a CLI a positional]]
2026-08-17 | cmd-cut-the-prompt-at-the-first-newline | verified-the-payload-not-the-transport | maintainer | yes-now | `open_terminal` spawned `cmd /c start "Command Center" cmd /k <argv>`, putting the whole invocation through two rounds of cmd parsing, and cmd treats a newline inside an argument as a COMMAND SEPARATOR. The card and note prompts are multi-line, so the CLI received line one and nothing else. Read out of the session transcript, 2026-08-17: the first user message was exactly 'Work this note from the board's inbox, with the maintainer at the keyboard.' -- and that is the worst possible truncation, because line one is note-SHAPED and names no note. The session did not ask which; it went to the inbox, chose `melee-crit-streak-not-interesting.md` itself, and spent 3m30s and 13.8k tokens analysing it and asking Karel three design questions, under a window titled with the note he had actually clicked. Its own recap read 'Goal: turn the inbox note about melee crit into a board card.' So the failure arrived as confident work on the wrong subject rather than as an error, and the only signal was Karel reading the title. Why it was believed: the prompt had just been verified, twice, at the wrong layer. Three tests asserted the prompt sits at `argv[-1]` behind a `--`, and it did. A headless replay ran the exact argv against the real CLI and had it echo back the card id, and it did -- but that replay called `subprocess.run(argv)` directly, which is precisely the path production does NOT take. The argv was right at every point I looked; the transform that destroyed it lived in the one function I had edited but never exercised, three lines from the assertion that the fence was in place. Both checks were designed against the previous bug in the same argv (`prompt-swallowed-by-a-variadic-flag`, this log, an hour earlier), which is how a check can be thorough about a value and blind to its channel. Generalises: TEST THE SPAWN, NOT THE ARGV -- when a value crosses a process boundary, the verification has to make the real call through the real launcher and read the value back out at the far end, because every reconstruction of the call omits exactly the layer under suspicion. The cheap discriminator, once suspected, is a six-line probe: send a multi-line argument through both spawn paths and count the lines that land -- one through cmd, six through CreateProcess. And the far-end read that settles it costs nothing here: the CLI writes every session's first user message to `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/<session>.jsonl`, so what the worker RECEIVED is on disk and never has to be inferred from what was sent. Fixed: no shell on the Windows path at all -- `subprocess.Popen(argv, creationflags=CREATE_NEW_CONSOLE)` gets the same detached visible window and passes the argv through CreateProcess untouched; an `OSError` now surfaces as a PanelError sentence instead of a console that closes before it can be read. Three tests, including one asserting no shell is in the spawned argv. Found by measuring while fixing it, and fixed with it: `CreateProcess` refuses a command line over 32767 characters, and this board already exceeds that -- `grid-distance-metric` builds a 32610-character prompt (largest of 109 cards), so that card's button could never have opened a session at all. `session_argv` now spills a prompt over a 24000-character argv budget to `.ai/runs/_panel/prompt-<slug>.md` and passes a one-line pointer to it. Deliberately left as the fallback rather than the default: a spilled prompt costs the session a Read before it can start. [[disposition: commit: CREATE_NEW_CONSOLE spawn + the argv budget spill, and transcript read-back as the verification for anything handed to a CLI]]
2026-08-18 | git-status-read-as-a-claim-about-disk | derived-not-verified | claude | yes-now | `deletion_sweep.removed_names` walked `git diff --name-status <base>..HEAD` and, for every `M` line, read the file FROM DISK to compare its top-level symbols against the base version. `M` states a relationship between two COMMITS and says nothing about the working tree, so a branch that edited a file and has since deleted it -- the ordinary mid-change state of any removal -- made `read_text` raise FileNotFoundError out of `check()`. `gates/run.py` does not isolate a gate's exception, so that stopped ALL 23 GATES with a traceback instead of reporting anything: `git rm tests/test_runner.py` on a branch whose earlier commits had edited it silenced the entire suite, and nobody would have read the absence of 'All clear' as a gate crash. Why it was believed: step 1 of the same function already handles working-tree deletions correctly, so the file HAD been buried and the author's model of 'deletions are covered' was true -- just not on the second path that also treats the same file as modified; and every existing test for this gate stubs `removed_names` out entirely, so the disk read had no coverage at any point and could not have been caught by the suite. Generalises: A GIT STATUS LETTER DESCRIBES A RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN COMMITS, NOT THE STATE OF THE DISK -- code that opens a path because git named it must ask whether the path is there. Second finding, not fixed here and worth its own work: one gate's unhandled exception can silence the other 22, which makes every gate's robustness a property of the whole suite. Fixed: an `is_file()` guard, plus a regression test built on a real repo and confirmed to fail against the unguarded version by reverting it.
2026-08-18 | per-fixture-cost-invisible-in-a-diff | cost-only-visible-in-aggregate | claude | yes-now | The suite rebuilt its whole world once per assertion: 32 hand-rolled `git init` + `config` sequences across 22 test files, each fixture measured at 467 ms (135 ms for `git init` plus three `config`, 85 ms for add+commit, 247 ms for `init.apply`) against 68 ms to copy a finished tree. `tests/test_update.py` ran 23 tests in 13.3 s with all eight of pytest's slowest entries being `setup` and not one of them a test body. Whole suite 1799 tests in 1018 s; after building each template once per process and handing every test a copy, 1819 tests in 174 s. Why it was believed: not one of the 32 sites is a mistake -- each is the clearest way to write that particular fixture, and 467 ms is beneath notice for whoever adds it. The cost exists only in the sum, and a diff shows one instance, so neither review nor any per-site check had a place to stand; the suite was simply known to be slow, for months, with no measurement of where. Generalises: WHEN THE DEFECT IS THE SUM AND EVERY INSTANCE IS CORRECT, REVIEW CANNOT SEE IT -- so the enforcement point has to be built at the SECOND instance, while building it still looks like over-engineering. Fixed: `tests/_fixtures.py` owns the construction, and the `fixture_rebuild` gate refuses a hand-rolled `git init` under the tests dir once a template exists -- it immediately found 8 sites a manual sweep of the same 22 files had missed.
2026-08-18 | bare-note-defined-as-having-no-frontmatter | derived-not-verified | claude | yes-now | `boardcmd.close_note` -- the only route out of the `inline` route, and the verb behind the panel's `Done` gesture -- refused any note whose text matched `board.FRONTMATTER`, on the stated reasoning that 'a file with frontmatter is a card mid-triage'. That is true of a note typed straight into the filesystem, which is how every fixture in `test_boardcmd.py` writes one, and false of every note on a board a human actually opens: Obsidian's Kanban plugin stamps `state:` and `kanban_order:` onto each card in the lane on every vault open, and `state:` is besides the field `reconcile` reads. Measured 2026-08-18 on the origin project: 7 of 7 notes in `inbox/` carried exactly those two keys, so `close` refused all 7 and the gesture could close nothing at all -- found only because a session was asked to close one by hand. Why it was believed: the discriminator was chosen against the fixtures rather than against a board, and the fixtures are the one population where a bare note has no frontmatter, so the test suite agreed with the guard for as long as both were wrong; and the failure is a REFUSAL with a plausible message, so the operator reads 'this is a card mid-triage' and looks at the note rather than at the verb. Generalises: A GUARD THAT TESTS FOR THE PRESENCE OF A CONTAINER RATHER THAN FOR THE FIELDS THAT MEAN SOMETHING WILL BE WRONG THE MOMENT ANY TOOL ADDS BOOKKEEPING TO THAT CONTAINER -- and if the fixtures build the container by hand, they will never contain the bookkeeping. Fixed: the guard now subtracts `_NOTE_BOOKKEEPING` (`state`, `kanban_order`) and refuses only on fields triage itself adds; the old frontmatter is stripped before the note's prose becomes `## Intent`, which it was not, so a closed note would have carried two `---` fences.
2026-08-18 | converged-versions-judged-against-history-alone | derived-not-verified | maintainer | no-judgment | `update.survey` classified each side against the RECORDED hash and never against the other: disk != recorded and template != recorded fell straight to `conflict`, with no branch asking whether the two live versions happened to agree. Measured 2026-08-18 on the Dungeoneer install -- `command-center.bat`, recorded 20bda7ed, disk 821a5a18, staged 821a5a18: BYTE-IDENTICAL to each other, verdict `conflict`, `update.diff` length 0. The panel therefore printed 'you edited this and the template moved -- nothing is overwritten until you say which wins' and offered TAKE THEIRS / KEEP MINE / MERGE, three inches from a DIFF button that answered `identical`. Karel, seeing both at once: 'It says project files are different but when I click diff button, it says that the files are identical... What does this mean?' -- so the defect surfaced as a report contradicting its own evidence button on one screen, which is the only reason it was looked at. Why it was believed: the module's own framing sets the question as 'how does each side relate to the receipt', and under that framing the three rows genuinely do exhaust it -- the docstring says a differing file 'is one of two completely different things', and both of those are true statements about the PAST. The receipt is a single point in history, so 'both sides moved' says where each came FROM and nothing about where either arrived. Two things then kept it hidden. The near-miss in the same class had already been found and fixed one layer down -- CRLF-vs-LF, two versions that differ without differing, solved by normalising inside `content_hash` -- which made the classification layer feel settled by association. And the fixtures could not produce the state at all: `_move_template` appends '## moved upstream' while `_edit` appends '## my own note', deliberately different strings, so all 24 tests exercised divergence and convergence was unreachable by construction. Worst of it: convergence is not an edge here, it is the ORDINARY end state of a framework that consumes itself -- an edit made in a project gets upstreamed, and every install then reports a conflict it cannot resolve. Generalises: WHEN THREE VERSIONS EXIST AND ONE OF THEM IS HISTORY, THE VERDICT MUST COMPARE THE TWO THAT ARE LIVE -- a recorded baseline can only ever say where each side departed from, never whether they are apart now. The mechanical tell is cheap and general: A VERDICT THAT OFFERS RESOLUTION VERBS WHILE ITS OWN DIFF IS EMPTY IS WRONG, whatever the reasoning behind it. And the fixture rule that would have caught it: when a test moves two sides independently, one case must move them to the SAME value, or the agreement branch is untestable and therefore unwritten. Fixed: `elif now == ahead` -> `current` ahead of the conflict fallthrough, the docstring's table grown the fourth row, and a regression test that hands `_move_template` and `_edit` the identical suffix and asserts no conflict, `current`, and an empty diff. `survey` still writes nothing, so the receipt stays behind until a verb touches the file -- costless, because the answer is re-derived identically every time. Verified on the install that found it: conflicts [] , changes 0, the 10 genuine `yours` untouched. [[disposition: commit: now == ahead -> current in survey(), with the converged-case regression test]]
2026-08-18 | decision-kept-in-the-view-not-on-the-thing | decision-kept-in-the-view-not-on-the-thing | claude | no-judgment | `ingest` wrote the classifier's answer for every note into `Routing.md` -- a view regenerated from scratch on each pass -- and onto the notes themselves never, so no note in `inbox/` carried any trace of having been decided. Four symptoms, all one cause, and none of them looks related to the others from where it surfaces. `classify` was handed the whole lane every run, so a note was re-read and re-decided for as long as it sat there: forever for the `triage` route, which nothing dispatches automatically, and Karel found it as a cost rather than a bug -- 'Definitely not running classifier multiple times.' `/now` listed inline-routed notes by consulting the view while `/inbox` listed every note by enumerating the lane, so one file was two rows on two pages with two different sets of buttons ('some cards are duplicated between now and inbox'), and a test existed asserting that duplication as a feature. Inline work never became a card at all, which put it outside every lane-reading view the board has. And `close_note` had to distinguish a bare note from a triaged one by frontmatter presence, because there was no field on the note that said what had been decided about it. Why it was believed: the module's own docstring argues the design explicitly and well -- 'Deliberately not a lane: the report is a view over inbox/, regenerated on demand... re-running is idempotent and there is no checklist state to lose' -- and idempotence of the ANSWER is true, so the sentence reads as settled; what it does not say is that re-running is not idempotent in COST. A `RoutingView` parser had already been built to let the panel read the view back, which made the view feel like a record precisely because something now depended on it. Generalises: A REGENERABLE ARTEFACT MUST NOT BE AN INPUT TO THE DECISION THAT WRITES IT -- if deleting the file would cost an answer rather than a render, it is a record wearing a view's name, and the two readers it acquires will eventually disagree in public. The mechanical tell is cheap: a producer that cannot name the subset it still owes work for without reading its own last output. Fixed: `route:` is frontmatter on the note, `classify` runs over `unrouted()` alone, the panel groups on `Note.route`, and an inline note becomes a card in `tasks/` immediately -- the view keeps only what a field cannot carry (the classifier's `why`, its confidence, its dispatchability) and nothing reads it to decide. [[disposition: commit: 7103923, one pipeline for every note]]
2026-08-18 | worktree-root-shared-by-every-fixture-repo | isolation-stopped-at-the-repo-boundary | claude | yes-now | `runner.worktree_root` is `root.parent / f'.{project}-worktrees'` -- outside the repo on purpose, so `doc_scan`, `card_schema` and the digest do not walk it. In the tests `root` IS `tmp_path`, so that resolves to `tmp_path.parent`, which pytest shares between every test in the session and, under xdist, every test on the worker. The project name is `dungeoneer` in every fixture manifest and the card id is `probe` in nearly every dispatch test, so all three components were constant and EVERY dispatch test in the suite resolved to one absolute path: `tmp_path.parent/.dungeoneer-worktrees/probe`. `tmp_path` isolated the repo and not the worktree, because the worktree is deliberately not in the repo. Nothing failed while every test cleaned up after itself; one that did not -- `test_an_api_error_before_verification_gives_the_attempt_back_and_hands_over` ends by calling `prepare_worktree` for an assertion about the returned MODE and drops the checkout, eleven lines after asserting that very path does not exist -- left the shared path occupied, and every later `probe` dispatch died in `prepare_worktree`: `git worktree remove --force` ran in the wrong repo and no-opped, then `git worktree add` refused a non-empty directory, so `dispatch` returned `failed` from its first statement, before the worker ran. Measured 2026-08-18 in preflight: 21 failures in `test_runner_dispatch.py`, all on `gw1`, every one reporting `failed` including the cases asserting `limited` and `interrupted`. Why it was believed: `tmp_path` IS the isolation primitive -- reaching for it is the correct instinct and it is right about everything the code writes inside the repo, so the fixture looks complete and the one resource it misses is the one the code was careful to put elsewhere. Two things then hid it. Serially the suite is ordered alphabetically, so `test_runner_dispatch.py` ran before `test_runner_review.py` and the leak always landed AFTER its victims -- four consecutive green full-suite runs, structurally incapable of catching it. And git's own message says only `Preparing worktree (new branch 'ai/probe')`, its progress line on stderr, never `the directory already exists`, so the one legible clue was absent. Generalises: A FIXTURE THAT ISOLATES THE THING UNDER TEST HAS NOT ISOLATED WHAT THAT THING WRITES OUTSIDE ITSELF -- and any path built from the fixture root's PARENT is shared by construction. The mechanical tell is a whole test file failing at once with an outcome none of its tests are about, which reads as a broken feature and is a broken neighbour. Fixed: `_worktree_repo` builds at `tmp_path/'repo'` so `root.parent` is the per-test `tmp_path`, plus two guards -- one asserting two fixture repos never share a `worktree_root`, one reproducing the leak-then-dispatch sequence -- both confirmed to fail against the unfixed fixture by reverting it. [[disposition: commit: _worktree_repo builds one directory down, with two isolation guards]]
2026-08-18 | deadlock-waited-out-for-two-days | waited-out-instead-of-attributed | maintainer | no-judgment | The xdist worker-respawn deadlock was known, reproduced and written up in `preflight._run_subset` on 2026-08-16 -- 'a worker died, xdist respawned it ~95s later, and the replacement never rejoined' -- and the response was a 2400s ceiling plus advice that 're-running usually succeeds'. Both are correct. Neither asks why the worker dies, and the flag that prevents the respawn entirely was never tried. Cost, measured 2026-08-18: Karel watched a preflight sit for 28 minutes on a suite whose healthy wall-clock is 3.7 min, and said 'This happens quite a lot lately, I need you to fix it. So it doesn't happen again.' Diagnosis took one five-test fixture whose middle test calls `os._exit`: default policy hung until killed at 90s; `--max-worker-restart=0` ended it in 0.77s, exit 1, reporting `worker 'gw0' crashed while running tests/test_crasher.py::test_kills_its_worker` and passing the other four. Why it was believed: the write-up is accurate and the ceiling fires, so the failure was survivable every single time -- and a precise explanation sitting in the code reads as a closed investigation, so each new occurrence sent the reader to re-read it rather than to re-open it. The parameter being a DURATION is the tell: it encodes an assumption that the cause is environmental and unreachable, which nobody ever stated or tested. Second finding, the reason it turned from rare to frequent and the part no single site could show: `suite.worker_count` adapts the worker count to free memory, so as this box's free RAM roughly halved (needs 12.4 GB for 6 workers, now has 6.8) the count fell 6 -> 2; under `--dist loadfile` that hands each surviving worker about half the suite instead of a sixth, roughly TRIPLING what one worker accumulates -- measured climbing monotonically at ~270 MB/min to 1.2 GB. So a latent per-worker leak became fatal with no change to any test, and the adaptive cap -- itself a correct fix for `guard-watched-the-wrong-meter` -- is what converted it. Generalises: A MITIGATION WHOSE ONLY PARAMETER IS A DURATION IS A CONFESSION THAT NOBODY ATTRIBUTED THE CAUSE, and the better the workaround, the longer that stays invisible -- so 're-running usually succeeds' should be read as an open bug, not a runbook line. Corollary: WHEN A RESOURCE PROBE ADAPTS PARALLELISM, IT ALSO CHANGES HOW MUCH EACH WORKER ACCUMULATES; a cap justified purely on peak memory silently moves the per-worker retention it was never measured against. Fixed: `suite.NO_RESPAWN` on every branch of `parallel_args`, with a test asserting it on each, so the respawn cannot come back through the capped path alone. NOT fixed and left open on purpose: the accumulation itself, which is the deeper cause and is now being hunted -- with it gone the worker count would stop mattering. [[disposition: commit: suite.NO_RESPAWN + test_a_dead_worker_is_never_replaced]]
2026-08-19 | second-dispatcher-inherited-work-not-obligations | category-of-one-read-as-a-literal | claude | no-gap | `chores` is a second dispatch path beside `runner` and inherited neither its reporting nor its preconditions; three symptoms, one cause, and each surfaces somewhere that looks unrelated to the others. (1) It opened no `run_record`, so `.ai/runs/records/` held nothing newer than 2026-08-02 while a batch ran 2026-08-18 11:17-11:41 -- and the Command Center reads the newest record to answer 'what ran', so the Run page put a fortnight-old NIGHT under the heading `Last run` with that night's roster under it, minutes after the batch wrote `Chores.md`. Karel, on the screenshot: 'it should provide the overview of what is running now. Not the run that was a week or two ago.' (2) `chores.eligible()` refuses on kind, tier, `unattended:` and attempts and never on `requires:`, which `runner.select` has enforced all along (runner.py:887) -- so the only thing keeping `ad-sound-for-recharge` (`requires: gpu-box`) off a laptop with no ComfyUI stack was that it ALSO carried `unattended: false`, itself a contradiction with `kind: chore` that a batch can never take. Fixing the contradiction alone would have dispatched an audio card on a box that cannot make audio: one latent defect was masking a second, and removing the mask was the obvious repair. (3) `panel.Context.chores` listed any non-dispatchable `kind: chore` card without asking `chores.eligible()`, so the panel advertised as batch work, under a `Run chores` button, the very card every batch reported as 'left out of this batch'. Why it was believed: `runner` was the only dispatcher when its obligations were written, so they exist as inline statements rather than as anything a second dispatcher could call -- one `run_record.start(...)` call site, one `requires:` test buried in `select`'s refusal chain. `chores`'s own docstring then draws the boundary explicitly and correctly -- 'Everything that drives a worktree, a merge or a suite run is `runner`'s -- this module calls into it and never grows a second copy' -- and that rule was followed to the letter. That is precisely what hid the gap: the WORK is genuinely shared, so the module reads as fully derived from `runner`, while everything that is not work was re-derived from memory and came out short. A sibling comparison comes out even on the work and uneven on the record of it and on the refusals. Nothing reported the absence either, because a record that was never opened has no file to be found missing and a precondition never checked raises nothing. Generalises: A SECOND IMPLEMENTATION OF A ROLE INHERITS ONLY WHAT THE FIRST EXPOSED AS A CALLABLE -- everything the first did inline is re-derived from memory, and the re-derivation is short in exactly the places that produce no output when they are missing. Corollary, and the reason this stayed invisible for four months: WHILE A ROLE HAS ONE IMPLEMENTATION ITS OBLIGATIONS ARE INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM THAT IMPLEMENTATION'S CODE, so the moment to name them is when the SECOND arrives, while naming them still looks like over-engineering. The mechanical tell is cheap: an entry point that dispatches work and writes no record of having done so. Fixed: `chores.execute` opens `run_record.start(kind='chores')` and re-states its outcomes at every transition; `eligible()` takes `capabilities` as a required keyword and applies `requires:`; the panel asks `eligible()` instead of matching on `kind`; and `card_schema` now refuses `kind: chore` with `unattended: false` so the masking flag cannot be written again.
2026-08-19 | receipt-keyed-on-identity-not-content | derived-not-verified | claude | yes-now | The preflight receipt recorded a commit's IDENTITY where it meant to attest to its CONTENT. `write_receipt` stored the SHA and `is_validated` matched on it, while every check the receipt stands behind -- `gates.run`, `audit --check`, pytest -- reads the working tree and nothing else: none opens a commit message, a parent or an author. The two coincide right up until history is re-parented or rewritten, which is precisely what the guarded operations do, so the proxy failed at the one boundary the guard exists for and nowhere else. Measured 2026-08-19 landing `ai/panel-activity-decide`: branch tip and `--no-ff` merge commit both at tree 254dad63, byte-identical files, and the push denied after a preflight that had just passed. Why it was believed: 'a receipt records that a commit passed' is the sentence anyone would write, it is what the module docstring said, and the code implementing it was correct in every case -- and the friction it caused was survivable every single time, because the response is 'run preflight again' rather than anything that looks like a defect. Nobody was ever blocked, only delayed, which is why months of merges paid it without anyone asking. SECOND HALF, and the more useful one, because it is about the diagnosis rather than the code: I reported this to Karel as '13 minutes of provably wasted work' and proposed the fix on that basis. Thirteen of those minutes were mine. I had run preflight with `--full-tests` both times, and `--full-tests` is DOCUMENTED as implying `--fresh-tests` -- 'every part actually runs' -- so it deliberately bypasses the per-part reuse cache that would otherwise have returned the identical verdict in seconds. Plain `python -m nightshift.preflight` would have reused it; the Dungeoneer half of the same landing did exactly that, in the same session, and reported 'board reused (27 test(s), tree unchanged)' -- evidence sitting in my own scrollback that contradicted the claim I was making. I measured a real number, attributed it to the system, and never asked which of my own flags produced it. Karel took the fix decision on my framing, so the framing being wrong is the actual cost here, not the minutes. Generalises: A COST YOU MEASURED WHILE DRIVING THE TOOL IS NOT EVIDENCE ABOUT THE TOOL UNTIL YOU HAVE SUBTRACTED YOUR OWN INVOCATION -- and the tell is cheap: before attributing a slow run, re-run it with no flags. Corollary about proxies: AN IDENTIFIER MAY STAND IN FOR A PROPERTY ONLY WHERE NOTHING CHANGES ONE WITHOUT THE OTHER, so a guard on the operations that break that coupling is the last place the substitution is safe. Fixed: the receipt records `tree` beside `sha`, `is_validated` matches either, and four tests on real repositories pin the boundary -- no-ff merge and amended message allowed, a REAL merge whose tree neither parent carries still denied, and a pre-`tree` receipt not becoming a wildcard through `None == None`. `--full-tests` semantics deliberately left alone: the coupling to `--fresh-tests` is a stated decision, not an oversight, and it was my reading of it that was missing.
2026-08-19 | reviewer-binary-verdict-had-no-room-for-mechanical-fixes | derived-not-verified | claude | no-judgment | The code-reviewer's verdict contract was reasoned out as strictly two-way -- `ok` or `needs_decision` -- and the code implementing it was correct for months. The first real card to hit a defect that was neither (a concrete, verifiable fact error with one correct answer, not a judgment call) exposed that the binary had nowhere to put it: `orientation-set-has-no-headroom`'s reviewer flagged a wrong commit attribution as `needs_decision`, and the "decision" Karel would have had to make was to run `git log` and read the answer off it -- a fact check, not a choice. Karel: "Shouldn't it run the card again instead? Or return it to tasks? ... What would you propose?" Why it was believed: "ok, or a human decides" reads as exhaustive on paper, because every prior finding really was one or the other -- nobody had hit a defect that was neither perfect nor ambiguous, just wrong and mechanically fixable, so the two-way split was never falsified until this card. Generalises: A ROUTING CONTRACT WITH EXACTLY TWO BRANCHES SHOULD BE CHECKED AGAINST "IS THERE A THIRD CASE BOTH BRANCHES ARE FORCING TOGETHER," not just "do these two cover every case I've personally seen" -- the forcing is invisible from either branch alone, because each individually looks correct on the cases that reach it. Fixed: a third verdict, `needs_fix` (nightshift@cb9830e5) -- the reviewer states the defect and its verified correct fix, and `settle()` sends the card back to `tasks/` for another attempt instead of parking it in `needs-decision/`, bounded by the same attempt limit as an ordinary `failed` retry, escalating to `needs-decision/` only if the same fix keeps recurring past that limit. [[disposition: commit: nightshift@cb9830e5, needs_fix verdict]]
2026-08-19 | view-restated-the-rule-the-code-decides | restated-the-rule-instead-of-reading-it | maintainer | no-judgment | Three of the six Command Center bugs Karel reported in one sitting are one bug, and the panel's own code contains the correct expression in all three cases. (1) The drag indicator: `insertBefore(held, after ? over.nextSibling : over)` decides before-or-after from the cursor's position in the row, and the CSS drew `.row.drag-over { box-shadow: inset 0 2px 0 0 }` -- a bar across the target's TOP edge, unconditionally. So every drop past a row's halfway mark was drawn one position above where the card went. Karel: "I see the green line at the right position ... but the card lands one position below that." (2) The parked-card chip read `sum(len(s.options) for s in decide.parse(...))` and was labelled `{n} decision(s)`, so a card asking one thing four ways announced "4 decisions" -- `len(decide.parse(...))` was the number the label named, sitting in the same expression. (3) `regionBusy` answered "is there user state here a swap would destroy" with "is the focus inside this region", and a `<select>` keeps focus after its own `onchange` -- so switching account made `#statusrail`, the one region that names the account, permanently ineligible for the refresh that would have shown the change. Why it was believed: each restatement is true of the case it was written for and cheaper than the reading. A top border IS the insertion point when you insert before; focus IS unsaved state when the control is a textarea; options ARE decisions on a card that asks one thing per option. The second expression is not written as a duplicate, it is written as the obvious rendering of a rule the author is holding in their head at that moment -- which is exactly when the rule is least likely to be re-read. Nothing forces the two to move together afterwards, and the display half has no test, because what would be asserted is that it agrees with the code beside it. Generalises: A VIEW THAT RESTATES A RULE IS A SECOND IMPLEMENTATION OF IT, AND THE ONE THE USER TRUSTS. The tell is a rendering whose condition is simpler than the code it describes -- an unconditional border under a conditional insert, a count with no branch under a parser that returns a tree, a `contains(focused)` under a question about what a control holds. The fix is never to correct the restatement; it is to make the view read the same expression the action reads, or to delete the view when the action already shows itself. Fixed: the drag line is gone, because `insertBefore` already moves the real row and `persistOrder` reads that same DOM order, so outlining the moved row IS the outcome and cannot disagree with it; the chip counts sub-questions and carries the option count as a separate clause; `regionBusy` asks `holdsUnsavedInput(focused)` instead of `region.contains(focused)`, and both rail selects blur themselves so neither half alone has to hold. The same discipline built the new tier control rather than being retrofitted onto it -- the chip on every card row and the `--model` the button passes both call `effective_tier`, which is the only place the override rule exists.
2026-08-20 | git-identity-stood-in-for-a-persons-name | derived-not-verified | maintainer | no-judgment | `{{maintainer}}` -- the token every agent charter and operator skill uses to address the person -- was read straight from `git config user.name` with no way to override it. On the origin project that value is the GitHub handle `karel-murgas`, so every rendered charter said things like "a choice only karel-murgas can make". Karel found it from the Command Center's System page, which listed five files as `you edited this and the template moved` and asked whether that should be happening. It should not have been five: the substantive template drift was real in all five, but roughly a third of the changed lines in each diff were the name and its pronouns, i.e. noise the operator would have had to read past on every file to find the part that mattered. Why it was believed: git user.name is the only name a tool can read off a repo without asking, the substitution is correct in shape, and the code implementing it was right in every case for the entire life of `init`. It was also unfalsifiable from inside `init`: a fresh install writes the charters once and nobody compares a new file to anything, so a wrong name there is a blemish nobody has a baseline for. `update` (2026-08-16) is what turned it from a blemish into a defect -- it re-renders every template on every survey and diffs the result against the installed copy, so the same wrong name is now regenerated and re-presented as a conflict, forever, and reappears on each future template move. Generalises: AN IDENTIFIER A TOOL CAN READ WITHOUT ASKING IS NOT THE FACT IT RESEMBLES -- a commit identity is not a name, an email local-part is not a handle, a directory name is not a project name -- and the substitution stays invisible for exactly as long as nothing re-derives it and compares. Corollary, and the reason this surfaced four months late: ADDING A COMPARISON TO A SYSTEM PROMOTES EVERY PRE-EXISTING GUESS IN IT TO A REPORTED DIFFERENCE, so the arrival of a diffing tool is the moment to re-audit what was being guessed, not just to read what it reports. Fixed: `[project].maintainer` in the manifest, preferred over git and falling back to it, plus a `discover.maintainer` CONFIRM proposal so a fresh install is asked rather than guessed at -- no gate can decide whether a given string is a person's name, which is why the mechanism is a forced question and not a check. `discover`'s own docstring, which had said "Three fields are here" since `board.decision_attributor` became the fourth on 2026-08-04, was corrected to five in the same change.
2026-08-20 | merge-delegated-the-write-and-misrecorded-it-both-ways | delegated-the-write-kept-none-of-its-bookkeeping | claude | no-gap | `update.merge` is the one verb of the four that does not perform its own write -- it hands the file to an agent -- and its bookkeeping was wrong in BOTH directions, each independently sufficient to lose the file it was protecting. (1) It recorded on `returncode == 0`. The first merge ever dispatched, against the Dungeoneer install on 2026-08-20, hit a write-permission prompt it could not answer in a non-interactive session, explained that clearly in its final message, and exited 0 having written nothing -- so `_record` stored the hash of the UNMERGED file. `now == was` on the next survey with the template still different is `stale`, which is in `APPLIES`: the file fell out of the set nothing may touch and into the one `--apply` overwrites. A verb that failed marked its own target safe to clobber, and the panel button that runs it says nothing about permission modes. (2) The success path was worse, and the test written for (1) is what exposed it: `_record` wrote the merged bytes into `created`, which means "nightshift wrote this here" -- so a merge that worked ALSO landed on `now == was` with the template still different, i.e. also `stale`, and `--apply` would have overwritten the merge an agent had just been paid to produce. Why it was believed: `take` and `keep` both do their own writing and both record correctly, and `merge` was written to look like them -- the sibling comparison comes out even on the work and uneven only on the record of it. Nothing exercised it either: `update` shipped 2026-08-16 with a test asserting merge REFUSES `permission_mode: default` before spending anything, which is a careful test of the refusal and leaves the path past it unmeasured, so the verb reached its first real invocation four days later untested end to end. Generalises: WHEN A CALLER DELEGATES A WRITE, ITS BOOKKEEPING STOPS BEING A CONSEQUENCE OF THE WRITE AND BECOMES A CLAIM ABOUT IT -- so it must be verified against the bytes on disk, never against the exit code of whatever was asked to produce them. Corollary about receipts specifically: THE FIELD A RESOLUTION IS RECORDED IN IS PART OF THE RESOLUTION, and the question to ask is not "what is now on disk" but "what did the operator just settle" -- a merge settles a template VERSION, exactly as `keep` does, so it belongs in `declined` and not in `created`. Fixed: the write is verified by comparing file bytes before and after, an agent that changed nothing returns non-zero with its own account quoted back, and a real merge records `declined: <template hash>` and leaves `created` alone -- so a merged file reads as the operator's own and stays quiet until the template moves again. Three tests pin it: the no-write path, its scratch-file cleanup, and the success path asserting the verdict is not in `APPLIES`. Nothing was lost in Dungeoneer -- `.ai/install.json` was still committed, so the corrupted receipt entry was restored with `git checkout HEAD --` and all five conflicts came back.
